#
# A client that switches bands on the same box is cut off for a minute, not
# for five.
#
# A non-MLO client (a laptop on 2.4 GHz) that authenticates on one link of the
# fronthaul AP MLD and associates on another leaves a stale entry behind.
# hostapd prunes it ("Prune association for <mac>") and 30 s later its
# DEAUTH/REMOVE timer removes the station by MAC - which also takes down the
# client's new association in the driver. The client stays associated but no
# data moves until ap_max_inactivity expires and hostapd drops it; then it
# reconnects. Measured 2026-10-01 on the office box, 2 of 2: Prune at T,
# "deauthenticated due to inactivity" at T+30 s, "disassociated due to
# inactivity" at T+300 s, internet back right after.
#
# 60 s instead of the default 300 s bounds that stall to about a minute. The
# fix itself (do not remove a pruned station by MAC while the same MAC is
# live on a sibling link) belongs in hostapd. Fronthaul only: the backhaul BSS
# has its own rule (98-easymesh-bh-no-inactivity-poll).
#
# This covers boxes upgraded with their settings kept. easymesh-genconfig
# (agents, every boot) and easymesh-role (a new controller) set the same value,
# because a box paired later rebuilds ap_mld_1 after this file has run.
# An explicit setting is never overridden.

if ! uci -q show wireless >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	echo "99-easymesh-fh-max-inactivity: no wireless config yet, will retry at boot" >&2
	exit 1
fi

uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1 >/dev/null || exit 0
[ -n "$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.max_inactivity)" ] && exit 0
uci set wireless.ap_mld_1.max_inactivity=60
uci commit wireless
logger -t easymesh-config "fronthaul: drop a silent client after 60 s, not 300 s (stall after a band switch on one box)"
exit 0
